1. Every victim of gender-based violence and children has the right to:
a. obtain protection for the security of themselves, their family and property, and be free from threats related to information that will be, is being, or has been provided;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. be free from ensnaring questions;
d. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions; and
e. obtain complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal assistance and law enforcement services, as well as repatriation and social reintegration services.
2. Victim rights as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 25

1. The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to implement efforts to protect victims of violence in the form of:
a. preventing the occurrence of all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children;
b. providing protection by providing complaint services, health services, social rehabilitation services, legal aid and enforcement services, repatriation and social reintegration services, seeking to increase community participation, as well as monitoring and reporting;
c. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
d. cooperating with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect, and recover victims of violence.
2. To carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and violence against children;
b. establish an integrated service center;
c. establish complaint service institutions at the sub-district level;
d. facilitate the implementation of integrated service centers and activities of complaint service institutions at the sub-district level;
e. provide facilities and infrastructure;
f. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;
g. coordinate and cooperate in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children;
h. encourage and increase community participation; and
i. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
3. In carrying out its obligations, responsibilities, and authorities, the Regent may delegate to Regional Apparatus.
4. Regional Apparatus receiving delegation from the Regent as referred to in paragraph (3) shall in its implementation prepare a Regional Action Plan for the protection of victims.
5. The Regional Government in carrying out its obligations as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives, or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 26

The community, family, and parents have the obligation and responsibility to:
a. prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children;
b. report if violence occurs;
c. protect victims; and
d. provide emergency assistance.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The Principles of Protection Administration for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children are:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of the victim;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. victim protection;
e. survival and development of children;
f. respect for the child's opinion;
g. openness;
h. integration;
i. empowering; and
j. victim confidentiality.

CHAPTER VI

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 27

1. The community may participate in assisting efforts to prevent and handle victims of gender-based violence and violence against children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. providing information and/or reporting any violence that comes to their knowledge;
b. providing protection for victims;
c. providing emergency assistance;
d. providing advocacy to victims and or the community regarding the handling of gender-based violence and violence against children;
e. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders; and
f. assisting in the process of repatriation and social reintegration.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.

Article 14

Legal Assistance and Law Enforcement Services as referred to in Article 9 letter e consist of:
a. legal assistance services including litigation legal assistance and non-litigation legal assistance;
b. law enforcement services including law enforcement at the police level, law enforcement at the prosecutor's office level, and law enforcement at the court level;
c. law enforcement services at the police level as referred to in letter b include law enforcement in the investigation and/or inquiry process;
d. law enforcement services at the prosecutor's office level as referred to in letter b include law enforcement in the pre-prosecution and prosecution process; and
e. law enforcement services at the court level as referred to in letter b include law enforcement at the pre-trial, trial, and verdict stages.

Article 15

1. Repatriation Services as referred to in Article 9 letter f include:
a. repatriation of victims of gender-based violence and children;
b. repatriation of victims of trafficking in persons.
2. Social Reintegration Services as referred to in Article 9 letter f include:
a. reintegration with family;
b. reintegration with substitute family;
c. reintegration with the environment; and
d. educational reintegration.

